<!--
  Nginx Proxy Manager (jc21/nginx-proxy-manager) — custom access log format,
  not nginx's standard combined log format:
    [27/Jul/2026:17:26:34 +0000] - 200 200 - GET https pve.example.lab "/uri" [Client 1.2.3.4] [Length 582] [Gzip -] [Sent-to 10.0.0.1] "UA" "referer"
  The native nginx decoder (program_name) does not match this format; worse,
  the format collides with the prematch of the native "zeus" decoder
  (0390-zeus_decoders.xml: `^[\d\d/\w\w\w/\d\d\d\d:\d\d:\d\d:\d\d \S+] `,
  which matches any line starting with a timestamp in brackets, whatever
  follows) — zeus captures it FIRST (loaded from ruleset/decoders, before
  any local decoder in etc/decoders, regardless of the order of
  <decoder_dir> in wazuh_manager.conf: tested, declaration order has no
  effect on matching priority).

  Solution: exclude zeus (decoder AND rules, otherwise the native zeus
  rules break the load by referencing a decoder that no longer exists) via
  <decoder_exclude>/<rule_exclude> in wazuh_manager.conf. Zeus (historic C2
  panel, cf. native comment) has no real use in this environment — an
  acceptable loss to unblock NPM.

  Regex gotcha (same limited engine as pfsense-nohostname.xml, see its
  comment): `[^"]*` (negative class) silently breaks the decoder (no error
  at load time, just 0 fields extracted) — brackets are ALWAYS literal
  characters here, never character classes. `\S*` does the job for
  capturing the URI (no spaces in a URL anyway).

  `status` is a reserved static Wazuh field name (load error: "Field
  'status' is static") — not used, see `id` below instead.

  `<type>web-log</type>` + fields named `id`/`url` (not `http_status`/`uri`):
  intentional. This makes the event inherit the whole native ruleset based
  on `<category>web-log</category>` (0245-web_rules.xml: 4xx/5xx errors,
  SQLi, XSS, LFI, CGI/PHP — 31101-31106, 31109, 31110) AND the local rules
  already written on top of it (100700-100702, domain
  `web,attack,web_attack_soc,` — command injection, web shell, confirmed
  attack) WITHOUT rewriting anything: they match on `<category>`/`<url>`/
  `<id>`, indifferent to which decoder produced these fields.

  Measured side effect, important for what follows: the native root rule
  31100 (unconditional on `<category>web-log</category>`) and our own root
  rule 100820 (`<decoded_as>npm-access</decoded_as>`) are two SIBLING rules
  without `if_sid` on the SAME event — only one wins (whichever loads
  first: ruleset before etc/rules), so 31100 always wins and 100820 never
  "wins" in practice. Consequence: the `rule.groups` tag "npm-proxy" carried
  by 100820 is never applied to the final alert. **Index routing
  (`wazuh-proxy-*` in alerts-pipeline.json) therefore tests
  `decoder.name=='npm-access'`, not `rule.groups` — the decoder itself
  stays stable regardless of which rule wins.** See
  rules/100820-npm-proxy-rules.xml for the rule-side detail (100823 chains
  on the native 31101, not on 100820, for the same reason).
-->
<decoder name="npm-access">
  <type>web-log</type>
  <prematch>[\d+/\S+/\d+:\d+:\d+:\d+ \S+] - </prematch>
  <regex offset="after_prematch">^(\d+) \d+ - (\S+) \S+ (\S+) "(\S*)" [Client (\S+)]</regex>
  <order>id,method,server,url,srcip</order>
</decoder>
